{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba sources/manuscript/main.tex | sed -n '1415,1545p'; sed -n '1,180p' SOURCE-CONTRACT.md; sed -n '600,700p' AristotleCandidates/CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean\"","aggregated_output":"  1415\t\\end{equation}\n  1416\twhich implies $x = 0$ and thus proves faithfulness.\n  1417\t\\end{proof}\n  1418\tNote that we have only proved faithfulness of $\\t_q$ as a functional on $qM_\\G\n  1419\tq$, not of $\\w$ $M_\\G$. The discussion preceding Proposition\n  1420\t\\ref{prop:faithful} needed faithfulness on the algebra to proof finiteness of\n  1421\tthe projection. In the current situation we can still prove\n  1422\t\\begin{corollary}\\label{cor:q_finite}\n  1423\tThe projection $q$ is finite in $\\cM_\\Gamma$.\n  1424\t\\end{corollary}\n  1425\t\n  1426\t\\begin{proof}\n  1427\tAssume the existence of a partial isometry $v\\in\\cM_\\Gamma$ with\n  1428\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:v_leq_q}\n  1429\t  v^\\dagger v=q,\n  1430\t  \\qquad\n  1431\t  vv^\\dagger \\leq q.\n  1432\t\\end{equation}\n  1433\tSince $v$ is a partial isometry together with the inequality in \\eqref{eq:v_leq_q} implies that the rang\n  1434\tof $v$ is contained in the range of $q$, on which $q$ acts as the identity. \n  1435\tThis implies that $v = qv$. \n  1436\tBy the same logic, the support of $v$ is contained in the support of $q$, so\n  1437\tthat $v = vq$. Therefore $v = qvq$, which implies $v \\in q M_\\G q$.\n  1438\tNow we can apply the argument preceding Proposition \\ref{prop:faithful}.\n  1439\tTraciality of $\\t$ implies\n  1440\t\\begin{equation}\n  1441\t  \\tau_q(vv^*)=\\tau_q(v^*v)=\\tau_q(q)=1.\n  1442\t\\end{equation}\n  1443\tTherefore $\\tau_q(q-vv^*)=0$. Faithfulness of $\\t_q$ and $q-vv^*\\ge 0$ then\n  1444\timply $vv^*=q$.  Thus $q$ is finite.\n  1445\t\\end{proof}\n  1446\tThe above proof verbatim also proves\n  1447\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:finite_corner_finite_global}\n  1448\t    Let $M$ be a von Neumann algebra and $q \\in M$ a projection. Let $\\t_q$ be a\n  1449\t    faithful tracial functional on $q M q$. Then $q$ is finite with respect to $M$.\n  1450\t\\end{lemma}\n  1451\t\n  1452\t\\subsection{Fullness and exclusion of type III}\n  1453\tWe now can move on to prove the main theorem. In the end, we want to\n  1454\tproof that for every central projections $z \\in \\cZ(M_\\G)$, the algebra $zM_\\G$ contains a nonzero finite projection. The projection we pick will be $zq$\n  1455\tand we therefore first neew to show that, for every central projection $zq$ is\n  1456\tnonzero. This goes under the name of fullness.\n  1457\tThe central support $c_{M_\\G}(q)$ is the least central projection in\n  1458\t$\\cM_\\Gamma$ dominating $q$ \\cite[III.1.1.5]{Blackadar}.\n  1459\tThe projection $q$ is called \\emph{full} if its central support is the identity.\n  1460\tFor a full projection $q$, if one has any nonzero central projection $z$, then $zq$ is\n  1461\tnonzero. If it were zero, then we would have that $q = (1-z)q$. For this to be\n  1462\ttrue, the\n  1463\tsubspace associated to the projection $(1-z)$ must contain the subspace associated\n  1464\tto $q$, which is exactly\n  1465\t\\begin{equation}\n  1466\t    q \\leq (1-z),\n  1467\t\\end{equation}\n  1468\twhich contradicts fullness, since $(1-z)$ is central and dominates $q$.\n  1469\tSimilarly, if $zq \\neq 0$ for all zentral projections $z$, then there cannot be\n  1470\ta dominating projection $q \\leq p$, since this implies that $(1-p)q = 0$, which\n  1471\tcontradicts that $zq \\neq 0$ for all central projections. \n  1472\t\n  1473\tTherefore, to claim that $zq$ is a nonzero finite projection, we need\n  1474\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:fullness}\n  1475\tThe projection $q$ is full in $M_\\G$.\n  1476\t\\end{proposition}\n  1477\t\n  1478\t\\begin{proof}\n  1479\tWe proceed by contradiction. Assume $q$ is not full. Then there exist a\n  1480\t$z\\in\\mathcal Z(\\cM_\\Gamma)$ that satisfies\n  1481\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:zq_vanishes}\n  1482\t  zq=0.\n  1483\t\\end{equation}\n  1484\tSince $q\\ket{\\W}=\\ket{\\W}$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists},\n  1485\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:zW_vanishes}\n  1486\t  z\\ket{\\W}=z q \\ket{\\W}=0.\n  1487\t\\end{equation}\n  1488\tSince $z \\in \\cZ(M_\\G)$, it $z$ commutes with both $\\pi_\\omega(\\cA_\\Gamma)\\subset\n  1489\tM_\\G$ and $\\pi_\\omega(\\cA_{\\Gamma^c})\\subset M'_\\G$.  Therefore\n  1490\t\\begin{equation}\n  1491\t  z\\pi_\\w(a)\\pi_\\w(b)\\ket{\\W}\n  1492\t  =\\pi_\\w(a)\\pi_\\w(b)z\\ket{\\W}=0\n  1493\t\\end{equation}\n  1494\tfor all $a\\in\\cA_\\Gamma$ and $b\\in\\cA_{\\Gamma^c}$. As described in the proof of\n  1495\tProposition \\ref{prop:faithful}, these states form a dense set. Thus $z = 0$\n  1496\tand there exist no nonzero central projection that is orthogonal to $q$. As\n  1497\texplained above the statement of the proposition, this implies that $q$ is\n  1498\tfull. \n  1499\t\\end{proof}\n  1500\tWe are now ready to proof our main theorem:\n  1501\t\\begin{theorem}\\label{thm:finite_projections_exist}\n  1502\t    Let $\\cS$ be a consistent stabilizer system on a countable lattice, $\\G\n  1503\t    \\subset \\cL$ a subregion, $\\w$ the state asserted by Theorem\n  1504\t    \\ref{prop:define_state} and $M_\\G$ be the von Neumann algebra associated to\n  1505\t    $\\G$ in the GNS Hilbert space $\\cH_\\w$. Then for every nonzero central\n  1506\t    projection $z \\in \\cZ(M_\\G)$\n  1507\t    the von Neumann algebra $zM_\\G$ contains a finite projection.\n  1508\t\\end{theorem}\n  1509\t\n  1510\t\\begin{proof}\n  1511\tLet $z\\in\\mathcal Z(\\cM_\\Gamma)$ be a nonzero central projection.  By\n  1512\tProposition \\ref{prop:fullness} $q$ is full, which implies\n  1513\t\\begin{equation}\n  1514\t  zq\\ne0,\n  1515\t\\end{equation}\n  1516\tas explained in the discussion preceding Proposition \\ref{prop:fullness}.\n  1517\tBecause $z$ is central, $zq$ is a projection\n  1518\t\\begin{equation}\n  1519\t    \\begin{aligned}\n  1520\t        (zq)^\\dagger &= q^\\dagger z^\\dagger = q z = z q\\\\\n  1521\t        (zq)^2 &= zq zq = z^2 q^2 = zq,\n  1522\t    \\end{aligned}\n  1523\t\\end{equation}\n  1524\twhere we used that $q,z$ are individually projections and by centrality $[q,z]\n  1525\t= 0$. The analogous calculation proves that $(1-z)q$ is a projection. Since\n  1526\tprojections are positive operators, we have\n  1527\t\\begin{equation}\n  1528\t zq\\le q.   \n  1529\t\\end{equation}\n  1530\t  This implies that $(zq) M_\\G(zq) \\subset qM_\\G q$. Since by Propositions\n  1531\t  \\ref{prop:faithful} and \\ref{prop:normal-trace} $\\t_q$ is a normal faithful positive tracial\n  1532\t  state on $q M_\\G q$. The restriction of $\\tau_q$ to the subalgebra\n  1533\t  $(zq) M_\\G(zq)$ is thus a faithful positive tracial functional, so $zq$ is\n  1534\t  finite with respect to $(zq) M_\\G (zq)$.\n  1535\t  Since $(zq) M_\\G (zq) = (zq) (z M_\\G) (zq) \\subset z M_\\G$ and $zq \\in zM_\\G$, $zq$ is also finite with respect to $z\n  1536\t  M_\\G$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:finite_corner_finite_global}.\n  1537\t  Therefore every nonzero central summand $z\\cM_\\Gamma$ contains a nonzero\n  1538\t  finite projection which proves the assertion.\n  1539\t\\end{proof}\n  1540\t\n  1541\t\n  1542\t\n  1543\t\\section{Short-range entangled systems}\\label{app:short_range}\n  1544\tThis appendix treats the special case of short-range entangled systems, where the stabilizers have a simpler structure,\n  1545\twhich allows the decomposition of the state into individual clusters. This allows more control over the resulting algebras.\n# Manuscript source contract\n\n## Authoritative source\n\n- Manuscript: `sources/manuscript/main.tex`\n- Title in source: *Infinite Magic and the Continuum* (`main.tex:117`)\n- Formalization policy: `AGENTS.md`\n\nThis project does not treat its own Lean files, prior agent prompts, or the\nsurrounding repository as mathematical source authority.\n\n## Source clauses in scope\n\n| Source locator | What the manuscript says | Formalization status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `main.tex:1671-1679` | Defines short-range order through a local symplectic basis, with `W_Gamma = span{z_i : i in P}` and finite support. | Requires a precise Lean model of the infinite Pauli/symplectic data. |\n| `main.tex:1740-1752` | States the short-range regional-algebra theorem, with an asserted factorization `A_Gamma` into a pure-sector type-I factor and a core factor that is finite type I or hyperfinite `II_1`. | Target statement; not established by current Lean artifacts. |\n| `main.tex:1915-1920` | Proposes an adapted symplectic basis and a star-isomorphism from an infinite Pauli algebra to `A_Gamma`. | External assumption/gap until the source construction is formalized. |\n| `main.tex:1927-1931` | Writes a pure/mixed product density state. | Finite density-matrix fragments are formalized; the infinite model is not. |\n| `main.tex:1945-1967` | Proposes the GNS/tensor-factor and type conclusions. | Conditional GNS transport is formalized; analytic product and type conclusions are not. |\n| `main.tex:1970-2004` | Proposes quasi-equivalence and normal-folium transport. | Not formalized. |\n\nThe wording above is a locator-oriented paraphrase, not a replacement for the\nmanuscript.\n\n## Permitted auxiliary assumptions\n\nAny manuscript-facing Lean theorem that needs one of the following must expose\nit in the theorem statement or task packet:\n\n1. a completed C-star or von Neumann algebra model for the infinite Pauli\n   system and its regional algebra;\n2. the asserted star-isomorphism from that model to the regional algebra;\n3. the product-state formula and hypotheses making the GNS representation a\n   spatial tensor product;\n4. the von Neumann closure, normality, and quasi-equivalence assumptions needed\n   to transport the claimed factor type; and\n5. any hyperfiniteness or trace theorem used for the core factor.\n\nNone of these assumptions is supplied merely by the phrase “short-range\norder” in the current manuscript source.\n\n## Trace correction\n\nThe manuscript-facing target must distinguish two cases:\n\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is finite-dimensional, a normalized tracial\n  state can be obtained from the finite matrix trace together with the core\n  trace;\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is infinite-dimensional, the appropriate\n  conclusion is a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight on the `II_infinity`\n  target, not a tracial state.\n\nThis is a correction to the intended theorem contract, not a claim that the\ncurrent manuscript already states or proves the corrected dichotomy.\n\n## Legacy firewall\n\nEarlier gLU/refinement/layer-coherence material is listed in\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`. It is retained, but it is neither a source clause nor an\nimplicit assumption of this formalization.\ntheorem central_mul_mul_central {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H} {z x y : H →L[ℂ] H}\n    (hz : M.IsCentralProjection z) (hx : x ∈ M) : (z * x) * (z * y) = z * (x * y) := by\n  rw [← mul_assoc, mul_assoc z x z, ← hz.commute hx, ← mul_assoc, hz.1.isIdempotentElem,\n    mul_assoc]\n\n/-! ### Central support and fullness -/\n\n/-- `z` is a **central support** (central carrier) of `p`: the smallest central projection\nacting as the identity on `p`.\n\nThis is phrased as a predicate rather than as a construction, so that no completeness of the\nprojection lattice (an analytic input) is needed. -/\ndef IsCentralSupport (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) (p z : H →L[ℂ] H) : Prop :=\n  M.IsCentralProjection z ∧ z * p = p ∧\n    ∀ w, M.IsCentralProjection w → w * p = p → z * w = z\n\n/-- A projection is **full** when its central support is `1`. -/\ndef IsFull (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) (p : H →L[ℂ] H) : Prop :=\n  M.IsCentralSupport p 1\n\nnamespace IsCentralSupport\n\nvariable {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H} {p z z' : H →L[ℂ] H}\n\ntheorem isCentralProjection (h : M.IsCentralSupport p z) : M.IsCentralProjection z := h.1\n\ntheorem mul_eq (h : M.IsCentralSupport p z) : z * p = p := h.2.1\n\n/-- `p ≤ z` for the central support `z` of a projection `p` of `M`. -/\ntheorem projLe (h : M.IsCentralSupport p z) (hp : M.IsProjection p) : ProjLe p z :=\n  ⟨by rw [← h.1.commute hp.2, h.2.1], h.2.1⟩\n\n/-- The central support is unique. -/\ntheorem unique (h : M.IsCentralSupport p z) (h' : M.IsCentralSupport p z') : z = z' := by\n  have h1 : z * z' = z := h.2.2 z' h'.1 h'.2.1\n  have h2 : z' * z = z' := h'.2.2 z h.1 h.2.1\n  calc z = z * z' := h1.symm\n    _ = z' * z := (center_commute h.1.2 (mem_of_mem_center h'.1.2)).eq\n    _ = z' := h2\n\nend IsCentralSupport\n\n/-- A full projection meets every nonzero central projection nontrivially: this is the basic\ncentral-intersection statement of comparison theory. -/\ntheorem IsFull.mul_ne_zero {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H} {p z : H →L[ℂ] H} (hp : M.IsFull p)\n    (hz : M.IsCentralProjection z) (hz0 : z ≠ 0) : z * p ≠ 0 := by\n  intro hzp\n  refine hz0 ?_\n  have hw : (1 - z) * p = p := by rw [sub_mul, one_mul, hzp, sub_zero]\n  have h1 := hp.2.2 (1 - z) hz.one_sub hw\n  rw [one_mul] at h1\n  exact sub_eq_self.mp h1\n\n/-- Conversely, a projection meeting every nonzero central projection is full. -/\ntheorem isFull_of_forall_central {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H} {p : H →L[ℂ] H}\n    (h : ∀ z, M.IsCentralProjection z → z ≠ 0 → z * p ≠ 0) : M.IsFull p := by\n  refine ⟨M.isCentralProjection_one, one_mul p, fun w hw hwp => ?_⟩\n  rw [one_mul]\n  have hz : (1 - w) * p = 0 := by rw [sub_mul, one_mul, hwp, sub_self]\n  have h1 : (1 : H →L[ℂ] H) - w = 0 := by\n    by_contra hne\n    exact h (1 - w) hw.one_sub hne hz\n  exact (sub_eq_zero.mp h1).symm\n\n/-- Sanity checks: `1` is full and `0` has central support `0`. -/\ntheorem isFull_one (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) : M.IsFull 1 :=\n  ⟨M.isCentralProjection_one, one_mul 1, fun w _ hw => by rw [one_mul]; rwa [mul_one] at hw⟩\n\ntheorem isCentralSupport_zero (M : VonNeumannAlgebra H) : M.IsCentralSupport 0 0 :=\n  ⟨M.isCentralProjection_zero, mul_zero 0, fun w _ _ => zero_mul w⟩\n\ntheorem isFull_iff_forall_central {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H} {p : H →L[ℂ] H} :\n    M.IsFull p ↔ ∀ z, M.IsCentralProjection z → z ≠ 0 → z * p ≠ 0 :=\n  ⟨fun hp _ hz hz0 => hp.mul_ne_zero hz hz0, isFull_of_forall_central⟩\n\n/-- For a full projection `p` of `M` and a nonzero central projection `z`, the compression\n`z * p` is a nonzero projection of `M` dominated by both `z` and `p`. -/\ntheorem IsFull.exists_nonzero_projection {M : VonNeumannAlgebra H} {p z : H →L[ℂ] H}\n    (hp : M.IsProjection p) (hfull : M.IsFull p) (hz : M.IsCentralProjection z) (hz0 : z ≠ 0) :\n    M.IsProjection (z * p) ∧ z * p ≠ 0 ∧ ProjLe (z * p) z ∧ ProjLe (z * p) p :=\n  ⟨hz.mul_isProjection hp, hfull.mul_ne_zero hz hz0, hz.mul_projLe_left hp,\n    hz.mul_projLe_right hp⟩\n\nend VonNeumannAlgebra\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
